 "result": "Both steps live in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n**1. Classes and the allowed methods**\n- `View` (L16) is the base class. `VerbView` (L138) subclasses it to dispatch by HTTP verb.\n- `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (L165) runs when you define a subclass. Unless the class sets `methods` itself, it collects `methods` from base classes and adds the uppercase name of each `get`, `post`, `put` etc. found in `http_method_funcs` (L11). The result is stored on `cls.methods`, e.g. `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n\n**2. Class to view function**\n- `View.to_view_func(name, *args, **kwargs)` (L86) is a classmethod that builds a plain function `view(**kwargs)`.\n  - Per request, it instantiates `view_class(*class_args, **class_kwargs)` and calls `run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n  - With `init_every_request = False`, it reuses a single instance instead.\n  - It applies `cls.decorators`, then copies `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` onto the function.\n\n**3. Routing to the handler**\n- `App.attach_route_rule` reads `view_func.methods` and `provide_automatic_options` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:622-643`), so the URL rule is registered for exactly the verbs the class defines.\n- At request time, `VerbView.run_view` (L182) does `getattr(self, request.method.lower())` and calls that handler. `HEAD` falls back to `get` if there's no `head` method.",
